CHAPTER III
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 5
Every Woman and Child victim of violence has the right:
a. to have their dignity and worth as a human being respected;
b. to obtain protection from family, community, Regional Government and other parties either temporarily or based on the determination of a Protection order from the Court;
c. to obtain recovery of physical, psychological and sexual health services in accordance with the suffering experienced by victims of violence;
d. to obtain special handling related to the confidentiality of victims;
e. to obtain assistance by companions and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. to obtain spiritual guidance services.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE OF REGULATION
Article 2
Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence, is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims;
e. local wisdom;
f. right to life, survival and development of children;
g. appreciation of victims' opinions; and
h. legal certainty.

CHAPTER X
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 15
(1) In providing protection to Women and Children victims of violence, the community may:
a. establish family partners at the kelurahan and village levels coordinating with Damang or Mantir Adat and Community Leaders;
b. establish Women and Children protection units within community organizations;
c. conduct socialization of Women's and Children's rights independently;
d. provide first aid to victims; and
e. report to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, family partners, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector, and mass media.

Article 4
The scope of Regulation on Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence includes:
a. rights of victims;
b. authority, obligations and responsibilities;
c. institutional matters;
d. minimum service standards;
e. social protection houses;
f. reporting;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. community participation; and
i. funding.

CHAPTER VI
MINIMUM SERVICE STANDARDS
Article 10
(1) MSS is implemented by the Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children with the aim of ensuring that Women and Children victims of violence receive the minimum services needed.
(2) MSS as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:
 a. handling of complaints/reports of victims of violence against women and children;
 b. health rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;
 c. social rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;
 d. legal aid for women and children victims of violence;
 e. recovery/psychological services for women and children victims of violence;
 f. mediation for women and children victims of violence; and
 g. repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of violence.

CHAPTER V
INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS
Article 9
(1) The established UPTD PPA can accommodate institutions/work units that provide services for victims of violence such as safe houses, social protection houses for Children, trauma center protection houses, women's social protection houses, shelters and other service activities in accordance with applicable Laws and Regulations.
(2) Forms of services implemented by UPTD PPA include:
a. complaint handling;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. legal assistance;
e. recovery/psychological services;
f. mediation; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration.
(3) The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with SPM.

Article 11
(1) SPM as referred to in Article 9 paragraph (3) has performance indicators including:
a. coverage of Women and Children victims of violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers at UPTD PPA;
b. coverage of Women and Children victims of violence who receive health services by trained health workers at Puskesmas and at Hospitals;
c. coverage of social rehabilitation services provided by trained social rehabilitation officers for Women and Children victims of violence at UPTD PPA;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Children victims of violence at UPTD PPA;
e. coverage of legal aid services for women and children victims of violence;
f. coverage of recovery/psychological services for women and children victims of violence;
g. coverage of mediation services for women and children victims of violence;
h. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; and
i. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.
(2) The determination of SPM performance indicators of UPTD PPA for Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) constitutes the minimum target that must be achieved.

